# Who reviews a change to the gates themselves.
#
# The Security workflow runs on `pull_request`, so it reads the .gitleaks.toml
# and the check scripts that the pull request itself carries. That is the right
# trigger — a rule change has to be tested by the rules as changed — and it is
# also the reason these particular files want a named reviewer: a diff that
# weakens them is graded by the weakened gate.
#
# Deliberately NO `*` rule. A catch-all would put every pull request in the repo
# behind one reviewer, which is a bigger change than this record is for. What is
# listed here is the gate's own definition and nothing else.
#
# CODEOWNERS is LAST-MATCH-WINS: a later line covering the same path replaces
# the owners of an earlier one, and a later line with no owner at all removes
# them. Append carefully. scripts/codeowners.check.mjs reads this file with those
# semantics and asserts every gate-defining file in the tree still ends up owned.
#
# This is inert until branch protection requires code-owner reviews. Today it
# names the reviewer and feeds the pull-request warning; it does not block a
# merge.

/.github/                 @asabirov
/.gitleaks.toml           @asabirov
/.pre-commit-config.yaml  @asabirov
/scripts/*.check.mjs      @asabirov
